AUDIO: 'In 2026, Americans are doing something unexpected in the design of their homes. They refer to the residential architecture of the 14th century Japanese samurai class. Not for aesthetics, but for the sake of the system. The'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '" buke-zukuri" courtyard complex, designed by warriors in feudal Japan, simultaneously solved four problems that the modern'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'American home still cannot cope with. Privacy without insulation, thermal efficiency without mechanical systems, spatial hierarchy without wasted space, and a connection to nature that is made'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'structurally impossible by a rectangular lot and a rectangular house. This video explains what the samurai invented and why American builders finally listened to'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. A residential tradition that four centuries of American building culture have never explored. This is affordable compact housing on a weekly basis. Subscribe now and click the'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'notification bell. Save this before viewing. The section on spatial hierarchy is the most useful material on this channel for anyone designing a multifunctional tiny house. Buke-zukuri is a'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'residential architecture of the Japanese warrior class that emerged in the Kamakura period in the late 12th century and reached its peak during the Muromachi and Edo periods, from approximately the 14th to the 18th centuries. It'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was built for a specific and demanding customer. The samurai household needed a residential complex that would simultaneously serve as a family'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'home, a training ground, a place to receive allies and rivals, a defensive position, and a spiritual retreat. Every spatial solution in the Buke-Yashiki complex'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'balanced these conflicting demands. The wall of the complex provided perimeter protection and privacy from the outside world. The main reception hall, the "shoin," was the'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'official space where the samurai conducted business and held ceremonies of their status. Private living quarters, " zasiki", provided an intimate family area. The garden, "karesansu" or'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '"tsukiyama", provided a place for contemplation where the samurai practiced the Zen-based warrior aesthetic. And the “engawa,” a raised wooden terrace that'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wraps around the perimeter of the main buildings facing the inner garden, created a threshold space between the interior and exterior, which Japanese tradition has always considered the most important in any building. Engava is'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not a veranda. This is not a corridor. This is not a garden path. This is the space between the inner and the'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outer, which simultaneously belongs to both worlds. A place where you can sit during the day and watch the garden change under the influence of light. Where you take off your shoes before entering the'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'house. Where the line between the protected interior and the open world of nature remains deliberately blurred . American builders, discovering'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'buke-zukuri in 2026, are discovering a completely different aesthetic from Japan. They are discovering a residential spatial system that solves problems that the American rectangular house has never been able to cope with. Today we'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will look at four solutions and the cost of implementing them in 2026. Save this section. These four issues apply to modern tiny homes in every American climate. The'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bukazukuri style addresses all four with a single organizational solution. Problem one: privacy without isolation. The American housing standard'

[03:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is either complete openness to the street and neighbors through conventional planning of setbacks and fences, or complete isolation behind a fence that cuts off the house from any connection with the outside environment. The wall of the bukazukuri complex'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provides complete external privacy from the street and neighboring plots. But the courtyard, which is surrounded by a wall, is connected to the sky, the seasonal changes of the garden, and the natural'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'acoustic environment of the site. The complex is simultaneously private from the built world and open to the natural world. Problem two: thermal efficiency'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without mechanical systems. The American housing standard is mechanical cooling and heating. Bukazukuri achieves the same thermal comfort thanks to four passive'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mechanisms built into its spatial organization. The wall of the complex blocks prevailing winds, which would increase heat loss in winter and reduce comfort in summer. The engava terrace'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shades the facade of the main building from direct sunlight. The gravel surface of a karesansuy garden reflects rather than absorbs solar radiation,'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keeping the yard cooler than any plant cover. And the shoji screen system allows you to simultaneously finely regulate light, air, and privacy in every opening in'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the room. Problem three: spatial hierarchy without wasting floor space. The American concept of open space combines all functions in one large room and'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'calls the lack of differentiation flexibility. Bukazukuri creates a spatial hierarchy through a sequence of spaces from the gate of the complex to the inner sanctuary without any specific area for'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'movement. The official shoin room differs from the private zashiki room in its location, proportions, and garden view, rather than its floor area. Every'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'square foot is a room. Spatial hierarchy is created by sequence and arrangement, not by the allocation of additional square footage. Problem four.'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Daily connection with nature. The American rectangular lot and rectangular house create a connection to nature that is visual at best. You can see the garden from the window.'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Bukazukuri makes the garden inevitable. The engava runs around the entire perimeter of the building, opening onto the garden. Each transition between rooms runs'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the engava and the view of the garden framed by it . Every morning, the garden is the first thing visible from the main rooms. Every seasonal change, spring moss on the'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'karesansui stone, autumn color of the maple, winter snow on the leveled gravel —all of this is visible simultaneously from any location in the building . Nature is not a feature of buke-'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'zukuri. It is the main organizing principle. Engawa is an element of buke-zukuri that American builders who use this system often describe as a discovery'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that changes their perception of every room they have ever built. This is a covered wooden terrace that wraps around the entire perimeter of the'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'main building from the garden side, raised 450–600 mm above the level of the courtyard , usually 900–1200 mm wide and protected by the overhanging roof of the building. An engava is'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not a porch, as it is not attached to the entrance of the building. It runs along the side walls and back of the building, opening onto the garden rather than the street. This is'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not a corridor because it has no ultimate goal. It connects each room to the garden without leading anywhere. You don\'t use an engawa to go from the bedroom to the kitchen. You'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use it to sit in the evening light between them. This is not a garden path because it is raised, covered, and has a wooden deck, as opposed to exposed stonework . Engava is a'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'threshold. A space that in Japanese residential tradition has always been considered the most important in a home, because it is the place where the boundary'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between inside and outside is kept open, not closed. In a modern American home, the boundary between inside and outside is always a'

[08:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wall and a door. You are either inside or outside. The threshold is the width of the door, just one step. In buke-zukuri, the threshold is an entire'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'room 900–1200 mm wide, protected from the weather, but not closed. Furnished with a wooden bench and a shoji partition that can be fully'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opened, partially opened, or completely closed depending on the light, temperature, and desired connection between the interior and the garden at a particular moment. Engava gives you a third'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'option between " inside" and "outside", which American doors never offer. The cost of adding engawa to a modern tiny house in the buke-zukuri style, a'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
